HEIMEL — name and origin

HEIMEL takes its name from the Norwegian word heimel, derived from Old Norse heimild.

A heimel is the legitimate basis for exercising a right or taking an action: authority, legal basis, title, or evidence supporting a claim.

That is the role HEIMEL plays for consequential authority.

HEIMEL is authority infrastructure. It is not limited to AI governance, governance teams, or human decision-makers. The actor may be a person, AI agent, software system, workflow, API, robot, or organization. The relevant question is the same whenever authority can become consequence.

HEIMEL does not decide what an actor is technically capable of doing. It determines whether a specific consequential action is authorized under the current authority, constraints and state — at the moment the action would become real — and preserves evidence of that decision.

The name captures the core chain in one word:

authority → basis → right → proof

HEIMEL asks the question that must be answered before capability becomes consequence:

What gives you the right to do this, now?
